From bc61502a49260daa34fa9251c038a6aaae2d4011 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 03:02:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] LinuxSyscalls: make translated-code pages writable before a
 kernel write (#5874)

With SMCChecks=mtrack a page that holds translated code is write-protected
and a guest store to it faults into FEX. A host syscall writing the same
page (read, pread64, recvfrom) cannot raise that fault: the kernel returns
EFAULT or a short count, where Linux fills the buffer.

Before such a syscall, invalidate the code in the part of the buffer that
the guest may write and that can hold code (writable executable mappings,
writable mirrors of shared ones) and make it writable, as a guest store
would. The syscall then runs once, unchanged: no retry, so nothing is read
twice, consumed early or blocks differently. Cost when unused: the
SMCChecks compare and one VMA lookup per call.

Test: eac-tests/smc-kernel-write (native x86-64: PASS x3) and
FEXLinuxTests smc/smc-kernel-write.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
